{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310863,"nonce":"zD0UF+rVyump0WqghCZ61eKA5qE=","title":"Decision: do NOT build SIMD4i (4xi32 elementwise is the WRONG SHAPE — quant hot path is a 16xi8-\u003e4xi32 FUSED widening dot). The RIGHT ...","message":"User asked \"Do we need SIMD4i?\" with the goal \"run quantized models, achieve superior latency.\" Investigated via workflow (quant-kernel-needs + int-vector-codegen readers, file-cited). Conclusion:\n\nThe hot quant kernel (Q4_K_M x Q8_K + Q6_K weight matmul, ~65k dot calls/FFN matmul) is a **16x i8 INPUT -\u003e 4x i32 ACCUMULATOR FUSED widening dot-product**, NOT a 4x i32 elementwise op:\n- Native AArch64: `vdotq_s32(acc_i32x4, a_i8x16, b_i8x16)` = SDOT, 16 i8xi8 -\u003e 4 i32, one instr ([sdot.rs:112](runtime-core/src/quant/sdot.rs)). Q4 fast path also `vmull_s16` (i16 widen-mul) for the min term (sdot.rs:327).\n- WASM: `i32x4.relaxed_dot_i8x16_i7x16_add` (-\u003eSDOT); fallback `i16x8.extend_low/high + i32x4.dot_i16x8` ([q4_k_m.rs:380,294](runtime-core/src/quant/q4_k_m.rs)).\n- Dequant is 16-lane byte ops: `v128.and 0x0F`, `u8x16.shr/shl/or`. Hsum: NEON `vaddvq_s32`; wasm extract+add.\n- SIMD4i would model ONLY the 4x i32 accumulator, never the 16x i8 multiply-reduce. The nue-plugins Q8 KV kernel IS f32 (dequant-then-f32x4) — already SIMD4f territory, not integer.\n\n- **\"Won't reach SDOT\" risk DOES NOT EXIST.** The SDOT lowering is shipped: darmie's PR #13640 merged upstream, rayzor on wasmtime 47/cranelift 0.134, production wasm objdumps to 24 sdot/0 smull ([[perf_wasm_v8_vs_wasmtime_2x]]). I'd cited a STALE smull-fallback (homebrew wasmtime-43 artifact) — the user built and shipped the fix. \"We own the runtime\" IS the strategy: the FFI kernel is the crutch to ABSORB into the compiler, not a permanent baseline to merely match.\n- **\"Capped at parity\" was WRONG.** The FFI call is an inlining/fusion BARRIER — memory's #1 wasm lever: \"Tier B CALL never inlined on wasm = THE wasm gap (L1)\", \"biggest wasm-decode lever = inline Tensor elementwise→SIMD4f+Ptr\". Behind FFI, dequant+dot+scale+elementwise can't fuse and marshal separately. Absorb into the compiler → they fuse, marshaling vanishes → path to SUPERIOR (not parity) latency FFI structurally can't reach. Parity is just the dot; the win is the surrounding collapse.\n- **Honest residual:** the dot is at NEON-SDOT parity both paths; the real gap is the Cranelift-vs-LLVM code-quality FLOOR on the ~30% NON-dot work (wasm no-addv hsum tax + weaker scheduler/regalloc + 2-acc ILP ceiling → ~30 tok/s wasm vs ~90 native). That floor currently favors LLVM-compiled Rust, but it's an UPSTREAM-cranelift problem (addv-fusion + aarch64 regalloc) — addressable, same as the user closed SDOT. Not fixed.\n\n- IR `IrType::Vector` is already element-generic (any int). The hir_to_mir VectorBinOp short-circuit (~19259) is element-generic too. Only `rayzor::SIMD4f -\u003e vector(F32,4)` is wired (hir_to_mir ~21810).\n- **Native Cranelift ALREADY lowers integer elementwise+reduce** (iadd/isub/imul/sdiv/band/bor/bxor over I8X16..I64X2, [cranelift_backend.rs:4442,5135](compiler/src/codegen/cranelift_backend.rs)). NO fused-dot/widening lowering.\n- **WASM backend HARDCODED to f32x4** for ALL vector arith (Splat/BinOp/Extract/Insert/Reduce ignore vec_ty, [wasm_backend.rs:3611](compiler/src/codegen/wasm_backend.rs)) → an integer VectorBinOp **silently miscompiles to F32x4Add / traps on and-or-xor. LATENT BUG.** VectorLoad/Store + V128 local-typing already element-agnostic; only arith arms are f32-locked. wasm_linker already maps the full int+relaxed-simd opcode set (I32x4DotI16x8S :2034, relaxed dots :2198-99) but ONLY as pass-through for precompiled Rust — unreachable from rayzor codegen.\n- Fused integer dot reachable from NEITHER rayzor backend (SIMD4f_dot is just Mul+reduce). Needs NEW IR op (VectorDot{acc,a,b,widen}) + Cranelift arm emitting swiden+imul+iadd_pairwise CLIF (which darmie's PR #13640 priority-8 ISLE rule CONTRACTS to SDOT) + wasm arm emitting i32x4.relaxed_dot_i8x16_i7x16_add_s (linker maps it → wasmtime 47 → SDOT). I.e. the downstream lowering is DONE; only rayzor's own backend op-emission is missing.\n\n- **Phase 0 COMMITTED (87a14a6e): wasm vector codegen now element-type-aware.** wasm_backend VectorSplat/BinOp/Extract/Insert/Reduce branch on vec_ty element → I8x16/I16x8/I32x4/I64x2 ops (was hard-f32x4, a silent miscompile for any integer vector). f32 byte-identical (SimdDemo, test_simd_e2e 17/17). `vec_element()` + `vec_elem_of_reg()` helpers.\n- **Phase 1 SIMD4i32 (i32x4): DONE, native+wasm (commit 69d44d13).** test_simd4i32.hx PASS both targets (make/+/-/*/get/sum = 50/100/11/14). Took TWO joint fixes: (1) **added `elem_ty: IrType` to VectorExtract/Insert/Reduce** — they carried no element type; wasm inferred it from register_types, LOST when a vector wrapper INLINES → emitted f32x4.extract_lane on an i32 vector → 0. MirBuilder builders already received elem_ty; wasm reads it directly; survives inlining (remap_instruction clones); non-wasm backends `..`-ignore (they lower by SSA value type). ~12 files. (2) **SIMD instance-method DISPATCH bug** (the actual masker): hir_to_mir hard-coded a `rayzor_SIMD4f` class hint for ANY vector receiver → SIMD4i32.sum()/get() dispatched to the SIMD4f f32 wrappers. Native masked it (Cranelift reduces/extracts by the i32x4 value type → right value by accident); wasm exposed it via elem_ty. New `simd_vector_class(ty)`: i32x4→rayzor_SIMD4i32 else rayzor_SIMD4f, at both hint sites + the SIMD direct-lookup. NO regressions (simd_e2e 17/17, tensor 20/20, native suite 139). Debug tool RAYZOR_DUMP_WASM + wasm-tools print cracked it.\n\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_simd4i_decision` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}